<?nvs
// The carrier a session rides on, as a program.
//
// [ADR 0059](../docs/adr/0059-cross-request-state-is-explicit.md) § 4 refuses
// `Core\Session` the local cache tier, and the reason is that tier's own
// contract: `local()` is per-core and in-process and may forget any entry at
// any time, so a session kept there would answer differently depending on which
// core accepted the request. The store half is Stage 6's. What already exists is
// the other half, and the half a forger attacks — the **identifier the client
// carries back**, and the fact that it is sealed rather than merely signed.
//
// [ADR 0060](../docs/adr/0060-application-security-protocols.md) § 1's
// `Core\SignedCookie` is that carrier, and its ring is ordered: `seal` uses
// `$keys[0]` and nothing else, `open` tries the whole ring in order, so
// rotating a key is prepending one and retiring a key is dropping the tail.
// The four lines below are that sentence asserted on both sides — a cookie
// still opens across a rotation, and the same cookie is a forgery once its key
// is off the end.
//
// `open` is also the one verification in the language that hands back a value
// with [ADR 0024](../docs/adr/0024-taint-tracking-for-injection-sinks.md)'s
// `tainted` removed, because the payload is one this application sealed itself.
// A signature verified against someone else's key does not launder anything.

// A ring of one, which is the ordinary case spelled `[$key]`. The key is
// generated rather than configured because what this fixture is about is the
// ring's *order*, and `[secrets]` would only make the same two keys harder to
// see.
secret bytes $key = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
array<secret bytes> $ring = [$key];

string $sid = "s-0f3a9c";
string $cookie = Core\SignedCookie::seal($sid, $ring);

// The round trip. The cookie itself is different on every call — each seals
// under its own nonce — so what is printed is the value that came back, never
// the carrier that brought it.
if (Core\SignedCookie::open($cookie, $ring) == $sid) {
    echo "opened", "\n";
} else {
    echo "opened a different value", "\n";
}

// The forgery, and the refusal that is what makes a session id worth trusting.
// Altered, not base64 at all, or sealed under a retired key are deliberately
// one message: telling them apart tells a forger which half landed.
try {
    string $forged = Core\SignedCookie::open($cookie . "x", $ring);
    echo "opened a forged cookie", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo "forgery refused", "\n";
}

// Rotation: a new key on the front, the old one still behind it. A cookie
// sealed before the rotation still opens, which is what makes a slow deploy
// possible without a hand-written fallback.
secret bytes $next = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
array<secret bytes> $rotated = [$next, $key];
if (Core\SignedCookie::open($cookie, $rotated) == $sid) {
    echo "opens across a rotation", "\n";
} else {
    echo "lost a session across a rotation", "\n";
}

// Retirement: drop the tail and the same cookie is a forgery, on the same
// message as any other.
try {
    string $stale = Core\SignedCookie::open($cookie, [$next]);
    echo "opened under a retired key", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo "retired key refused", "\n";
}
